<%- include('../partials/header') %>

<div class="page-head">
  <h1>Import members from a spreadsheet</h1>
  <p class="sub">
    The parish already holds its families somewhere &mdash; a register, a
    ledger, somebody&rsquo;s spreadsheet. Nobody should type it in twice.
    Download the sheet below, fill it in, and hand it to whoever looks after
    this installation to load.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h2>Get the template</h2>
  <p class="help" style="margin-top:0">
    A comma-separated file, which Excel, LibreOffice and Google Sheets all open
    by double-clicking. Fill it in there and save it back as
    <code>.csv</code> &mdash; save it as <code>.xlsx</code> and it can no
    longer be read.
  </p>

  <div class="actions">
    <a class="btn" href="<%= base %>/admin/import-template.csv">Template with example rows</a>
    <a class="btn ghost" href="<%= base %>/admin/import-template.csv?examples=0">
      Headings only
    </a>
  </div>

  <p class="help">
    The example rows are there to be read and then deleted. The headings-only
    file is the same sheet with nothing under them.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h2>The one rule that matters</h2>
  <p class="help" style="margin-top:0">
    <strong>One row per person, not one row per family.</strong> A family of
    five is five rows carrying the same <strong>Family ID</strong>, and that
    column is what ties them together. It is the only column the import
    refuses a sheet without.
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    The family&rsquo;s own details &mdash; address, prayer group, date of
    marriage &mdash; are read from the <em>first</em> row carrying that Family
    ID. Leave them blank on the rest of the household, or repeat them; the
    sheet is not wrong either way.
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    Two families must never share a Family ID, and a Family ID already in this
    parish is reported as already there and left untouched &mdash; a correction
    the office has made by hand is never undone by an import somebody runs
    twice.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h2>The columns</h2>
  <p class="help" style="margin-top:0">
    Column order does not matter, and neither does letter case, spacing or
    punctuation in the headings &mdash; &ldquo;Family ID&rdquo;,
    &ldquo;family_id&rdquo; and &ldquo;FAMILY&nbsp;&nbsp;ID.&rdquo; are one
    column. If the parish sheet already calls a column something else, check
    the alternatives here before renaming anything: it is probably recognised
    as it stands. A column that is recognised by no heading at all is reported
    when the sheet is read, never dropped in silence.
  </p>

  <table class="table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Heading in the template</th>
        <th>Also accepted as</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% fields.forEach((field) => { %>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <strong><%= columnLabels[field] %></strong>
            <% if (required.includes(field)) { %>
              <span class="tag">required</span>
            <% } %>
          </td>
          <td class="help">
            <%= aliases[field].filter((a) => a !== columnLabels[field].toLowerCase()).join(', ') %>
          </td>
        </tr>
      <% }) %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h2>Filling in the awkward columns</h2>

  <p class="help" style="margin-top:0">
    <strong>Relation.</strong> Use this parish&rsquo;s own codes, the same ones
    the member editor offers:
    <% if (relations.length) { %>
      <% relations.forEach((relation, i) => { %><code><%= relation %></code><%= i < relations.length - 1 ? ', ' : '' %><% }) %>.
    <% } else { %>
      none are set &mdash; add them on the
      <a href="<%= base %>/admin/settings">Settings</a> page first.
    <% } %>
    Change them on Settings and the template you download changes with them.
  </p>

  <p class="help">
    <strong>Dates.</strong> Write them however the register has them.
    <code>02-Aug-1975</code>, <code>2/8/1975</code>, <code>1975-08-02</code>
    and <code>Aug 1975</code> are all read, and where two numbers are
    ambiguous the <em>day comes first</em> &mdash; <code>03/04/1978</code> is
    the third of April. A date of birth with no year keeps its day and month,
    and the birthday still prints. A date that cannot be read at all is
    reported as a rejected row rather than quietly blanked.
  </p>

  <p class="help">
    <strong>Date of marriage.</strong> This directory prints the day and month
    only. Write the year if the register has it; it is read and then dropped,
    not refused.
  </p>

  <p class="help">
    <strong>Head of family.</strong> If it is blank, the first member listed
    for that family is taken as the head, and the import says so. A family with
    no head and no members is refused &mdash; a printed entry has to have a
    title.
  </p>

  <p class="help">
    <strong>Address.</strong> A line break inside the cell is fine &mdash; the
    two-line address you get from pressing Alt+Enter in Excel survives intact.
  </p>

  <p class="help">
    <strong>Photographs</strong> are not part of this. They are added to each
    family afterwards, from the family&rsquo;s own page.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h2>Handing the sheet over</h2>
  <p class="help" style="margin-top:0">
    The load itself is run at the command line by whoever installed this, once,
    against this parish:
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    <code>npm run import-families -- --church <%= church.slug %> --file parish.csv --dry-run</code>
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    The dry run writes nothing. It reports every family it would create and
    every row it cannot read, so the sheet is corrected before anything enters
    the directory. Running it again without <code>--dry-run</code> does the
    load; adding <code>--rejects bad.csv</code> writes out exactly the rows
    that failed, with the reason in the last column, ready to be fixed and
    imported again.
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    A family with any unreadable row is held back whole, so a single bad date
    never leaves a family half-imported. Everything that does arrive arrives as
    a <strong>draft</strong> &mdash; nothing enters the printed book until
    somebody has looked at it &mdash; and <strong>no logins are created</strong>.
    Accounts stay a separate, deliberate step on
    <a href="<%= base %>/admin/users">User accounts</a>.
  </p>
</div>

<%- include('../partials/footer') %>
